    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Tutoring is a vital educational tool and a tu- tor’s attention is a crucial teaching resource for promoting more equitable and effective learn- ing. In small group tutoring, instructors pro- vide individualized attention to foster authentic engagement and mastery learning. In the mean- time, instructing multiple students requires the tutor to skillfully direct their attention to fa- cilitate collective learning and ensure equity among students. In this study, we develop a \"split or share\" (SoS) framework to measure instructional attention in small group settings. We apply natural language processing methods to automatically identify whom an instructor talks to and fine-tune a classier to achieve per- formance comparable to human categorization. Our findings reveal that tutors spend an average of 37.60% of their speaking time addressing both students simultaneously, suggesting that tutors split their attention across students to a certain degree in 2:1 sessions. Substantial varia- tion across sessions and tutors indicates diverse pedagogical styles and potential inequities in student engagement. Linguistic analyses show that tutors use subject-specific and task-related words when addressing both students, and more spoken assent or affect but also technical man- agement words when addressing individual stu- dents. Our approach highlights the potential of computational linguistics in making inferences about cognitive constructs like attention and providing a more fine-grained understanding of instructional practice.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Zhang, Q. (2024). Share or Split: Understand Allocation of Tutor Attention Across Students via Discourse in Small-Group Tutoring. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/cb743gk8141. https://doi.org/10.25740/cb743gk8141.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
